<h1>About Crystopedia</h1>
<p>Crystopedia brings every IMA mineral species together with the popular crystals, gems and fossils collectors love. Mineral data comes from Wikidata; folklore is kept clearly separate from science.</p>
<h2>Reading a stone page</h2>
<ul>
<li><span class="holo-num" style="position:static;display:inline-grid">1</span> Holographic crystal points number the stones in each list.</li>
<li><strong>Did You Know?</strong> bubbles are built only from that page's own data (type locality, UV response, density, hardness, birthstone). Stones without a usable fact simply don't get one.</li>
<li>The <strong>Mohs rainbow gauge</strong> runs from 1 (talc) to 10 (diamond); the outlined band shows the stone's hardness range.</li>
<li><span class="tiffany-pick" style="font-size:1.1rem;padding:2px 10px">Tiffany's Pick</span> marks stones photographed from Tiffany Stark's own collection.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="rarity">How rarity is scored</h2>
<p>True geological abundance isn't recorded for most minerals, so the 1–5 crystal meter is a <strong>proxy for how often a collector is likely to come across the stone</strong>:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Common</strong>: widely sold in crystal shops (it's in our healing-crystal list).</li>
<li><strong>Easy to find</strong>: an established gem material or named variety.</li>
<li><strong>Collector stone</strong>: a species with both a Wikipedia article and a Wikimedia Commons photo.</li>
<li><strong>Rare</strong>: only one of those.</li>
<li><strong>Very rare</strong>: neither; it's documented almost only in the scientific literature.</li>
</ol>
<p>A short list of famously rare gems (benitoite, painite, red beryl, grandidierite, musgravite, jeremejevite, taaffeite, poudretteite at 5; alexandrite, tanzanite, Paraíba tourmaline, padparadscha, demantoid and black opal at 4) is set by hand. Treat the meter as a guide, not a measurement.</p>
